<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="adenosine ribonucleotides de novo biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7219" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The first purine nucleotide that is synthesized &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; is |FRAME: IMP| (IMP). IMP is converted to |FRAME: ADENYLOSUCC| by the enzyme |FRAME: EC-6.3.4.4|, and the latter is converted to the first adenosine nucleotide, |FRAME: AMP &quot;adenosine 5&apos;-monophosphate&quot;| (AMP) by the action of |FRAME: EC-4.3.2.2|. AMP is phosphorylated to ADP and subsequently to ATP. The first reaction is catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-2.7.4.3|. Reactions that convert ADP to ATP are numerous and found in multiple pathways, including fueling pathways such as anaerobic respiration, TCA-cycle based aerobic respiration, fermentation, and glycolysis, and thus this conversion is not included in this pathway. A major source for this conversion is the |FRAME: CPLX490-3510 ATP synthase| complex (|FRAME: EC-7.1.2.2 &quot;EC 3.6.3.14&quot;|), which is membrane bound and utilizes the proton gradient across membranes to phosphorylate ADP. That enzyme is described in pathway |FRAME: PWY-7980|. In some bacteria ADP can also be converted to the deoxy form |FRAME: DATP| (see |FRAME:PWY-7220|). Genetic studies indicate that in bacterial systems the majority of &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; purine biosynthetic genes are unlinked but may act as a single unit of regulation controlled by the |FRAME: PC00033| |CITS:[2404765]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
